{
  "id": "lesson-ef-007",
  "slug": "entity-framework-core-conventions",
  "moduleSlug": "conventions-relationships",
  "title": "Entity Framework Core Conventions",
  "description": "Learn about default conventions for schema, tables, columns, keys, and data types.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedMinutes": 28,
  "tags": ["ef-core", "conventions", "schema", "mapping"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Conventions are default rules using which Entity Framework builds a model based on your domain classes. EF Core API creates a database schema based on these conventions without additional configuration."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Schema Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates all database objects in the **dbo** schema by default."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Table Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates tables for all `DbSet<TEntity>` properties with the same name as the property. It also creates tables for entities reachable through reference properties."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Column Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates columns for all scalar properties with the same name as the property."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Data Type Mapping"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["C# Data Type", "SQL Server Data Type"],
        "rows": [
          ["int", "int"],
          ["string", "nvarchar(Max)"],
          ["decimal", "decimal(18,2)"],
          ["float", "real"],
          ["byte[]", "varbinary(Max)"],
          ["datetime", "datetime"],
          ["bool", "bit"],
          ["byte", "tinyint"],
          ["short", "smallint"],
          ["long", "bigint"],
          ["double", "float"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Primary Key Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates a primary key for properties named `Id` or `<EntityClassName>Id` (case insensitive)."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Foreign Key Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates foreign key columns for reference navigation properties with these naming patterns:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "`<ReferenceNavigationPropertyName>Id`",
          "`<ReferenceNavigationPropertyName><PrincipalPrimaryKeyPropertyName>`"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Index Convention"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "EF Core creates a clustered index on primary key columns and a non-clustered index on foreign key columns by default."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Schema defaults to dbo",
          "Table names match DbSet property names",
          "Column names match property names",
          "Primary key: Id or EntityNameId pattern",
          "Foreign key: NavigationPropertyNameId pattern"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Continue to learn about relationship conventions."
      }
    }
  ],
  "relatedLessons": [
    "one-to-many-relationships-conventions",
